# Question: should we commit to a year of reserved capacity or a savings
# plan when we are not sure how much we will use, and how likely is the
# commitment to come out cheaper than paying on demand?
#
# Run it:
#   ubc --scenario cloud/reserved-capacity
# Change an assumption, for example a usage that may shrink after a migration:
#   ubc --set 'usage = 20k to 60k' --scenario cloud/reserved-capacity
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens savings --scenario cloud/reserved-capacity
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. Amounts are in dollars a
# month, at on-demand prices. The commitment is paid whether it is used or
# not, at a discount; usage above it is paid on demand.
#
# Sources: public price lists of the cloud providers (AWS, Google Cloud,
# Azure), for commitment discounts; the usage range comes from your own bill.

# Assumptions
usage = 30k to 80k                # average monthly compute over the year, at on-demand prices; from your bill
commitment = 50k                  # the monthly usage we commit to, at on-demand prices; the option being weighed
discount = 20% to 35%             # discount for committing for a year; check your provider's public price list

# Calculation
on_demand = usage                                                     # paying on demand for everything
committed = commitment * (1 - discount) + max(usage - commitment, 0)  # the commitment, plus any usage above it
savings = (on_demand - committed) * 12                                # a year of savings; negative when the commitment is wasted

# Result
savings
P(usage < commitment)             # how likely are we to pay for capacity we do not use?
P(savings > 0)                    # how likely is the commitment to come out cheaper?
